<Project>

  <!-- Inherit the repo-wide identity/packaging defaults, then layer on the
       settings that apply only to shipping library assemblies. -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup Label="Packaging">
    <!-- Everything under src/ is a shipping library. Individual projects may
         opt out with <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <Description>Tetitulz.Pdf — a pure-managed .NET PDF library (no native PDF engine).</Description>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Packaging" Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      The package page shows the project's OWN README when it has one, and the repository README
      otherwise. Both are packed as README.md, which is what PackageReadmeFile above names.

      Until 2026-09-05 this packed the repository README unconditionally, so the seven projects
      that had written their own shipped it and nobody ever saw theirs - the file was in the repo,
      referenced from the docs, and absent from the package. Verified by unzipping a .nupkg and
      reading the first line, not by reading this file.
    -->
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/README.md')" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false"
          Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/README.md')" />
    <!--
      The third-party notices travel with every package: the dependencies' and the embedded data's
      licences ask for their notice "with all copies" (MIT, BSD, Apache 2.0 section 4, the Unicode
      licence). A project embedding data adds that data's full licence text beside these.
    -->
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../NOTICE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../NOTICE.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../THIRD-PARTY.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="API documentation">
    <!--
      The library already carries ~1,400 authored /// doc comments that were
      never reaching consumers because no project emitted an XML doc file.
      CS1591 (missing doc comment on a public member) is suppressed: coverage is
      partial (Base 1094/1392, Core 321/987) and turning ~950 warnings on would
      drown real diagnostics. Remove the NoWarn once coverage is complete.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>

    <!--
      A BROKEN doc tag is a different thing from a MISSING one, and these five
      fail the build.

      CS1570 is the one that costs most: badly formed XML makes the compiler drop
      the WHOLE comment from the emitted .xml, so a carefully written block simply
      does not reach consumers and nothing says so. CS1734/CS1572 (a paramref or
      param naming a parameter that is not there) is the tell for a doc block that
      has drifted onto the wrong member — six were found that way, each one a
      method whose real documentation was sitting above a different method and a
      method left with none. CS1574/CS0419 point a reader at something that does
      not exist, or silently at the wrong overload.

      Set once the whole tree measured clean across every target framework
      (127 sites closed). Do NOT add one of these to NoWarn to get green:
      point the tag at what exists, or delete the stale cross-reference.

      CS1573 (some parameters documented, others not) WAS deliberately absent here —
      219 live sites, a partial-coverage problem of the same kind as CS1591. That
      reasoning expired on 2026-08-28 when the count reached 0; it now fails the
      build alongside CS1571/CS1587/CS0105, in Directory.Build.targets. It is also
      not the cosmetic class it looked like: XML doc params are all-or-nothing, so
      documenting one parameter converts every sibling from undocumented-and-quiet
      to undocumented-and-warned.

      The nullable family lives in that same targets file rather than here, because
      it is conditioned on <Nullable> and a .props is evaluated before the project
      sets it.
    -->
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS0419;CS1570;CS1572;CS1574;CS1734</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT set here: <Nullable>.

    The plan called for <Nullable>annotations</Nullable> repo-wide, but that is
    wrong for this codebase and would make the public API *less* honest, not more.
    With no `?` annotations anywhere in src/, `annotations` mode emits
    [NullableContext(1)] and marks every reference type NON-nullable in metadata —
    which would tell consumers that the ~24 documented "returns null on failure"
    methods (PdfParser, PdfStructuredTextExtractor, PdfFieldExtractor, …) never
    return null. Nullable-oblivious is the safer state until the annotations are
    actually written.

    The correct path is per-project <Nullable>enable</Nullable> plus real
    annotation work, starting with Tetitulz.Pdf.Core. Tracked as Tier 2.
  -->

</Project>
